<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.18.7: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01200</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.18.7: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4212ea73-b707-412c-a743-2325f9cf6469</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="a2ed7149-25ab-40c4-a28b-e471cbb510fe" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--1a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="99402f9a-a336-4a0e-a2d3-09edacc50f47" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="163">
    <problem>
      <para>Find the value of each specified term of the arithmetic sequences below. Hint: Enter your answers as an integer only. Do not include commas or periods.</para>
        <para>Find the \(21^{st}\) term of a sequence where the first term is \(3\) and the common difference is \(8\).
          </para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 163</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 and 4.18.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="ca8da07c-a1b2-47c5-8fc9-4f00d8795c60" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="131">
    <problem>
      <para>Find the \(30^{th}\) term of a sequence where the first term is \(–14\) and the common difference is \(5\).
          </para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(131\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 and 4.18.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="59a632cd-0c67-4b5d-abab-0c35a39ba7e3" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-79">
    <problem>
      <para>Find the \(16^{th}\) term of a sequence where the first term is \(11\) and the common difference is \(–6\).
          </para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! –79</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 and 4.18.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="39bd974f-5453-4265-8b24-c1864b65633f" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="472392">
    <problem>
      <para>Find the value of each specified term of the geometric sequences below. Hint: Enter your answers as integers only. Do not include commas or periods.</para>
        <para>Find the \(11^{th}\) term given the first term is \(8\) and the common ratio is \(3\).
          </para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(472392\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1ebdbd24-382d-4498-a97e-36145e5f04d2" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="3072">
    <problem>
      <para>Find the \(10^{th}\) term given the first term is \(–6\) and the common ratio is \(–2\).
          </para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 3072</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="baf6f255-ad47-4bf3-8bcf-4ed32dbc4b92" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="\(a_{n}=-6+(n-1)(-2) \)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(a_{n}=-2+(n-1)(-6) \\)&quot;, &quot;\\(a_{n}=-6+(n-1)(-2) \\)&quot;, &quot;\\(a_{n}=-6+(n-1)+(-2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(a_{n}=-6+(n)(-2)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A sequence is defined recursively by \(f_1=-6, f_n=f_{n-1}-2\), for \(n≥2\). Which of the following gives the \(n^{th}\) term for \(n≥0\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(a_{n}=-6+(n-1)(-2) \)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="38258be5-0b3a-4ef7-ab27-c604a6fdace1" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="\(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)(5)\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)+5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)(-2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)(5)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(a_{n}=5+(n-1)(-20)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A sequence is defined recursively by \(f_1=-20, f_n=f_{(n-1)}+5\) for \(n≥2\). Which of the following is the \(n^{th}\) term?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)(5)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="6fffcf18-e6ac-4983-9fe7-1963ef7b5157" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(f_n=3 \cdot 2^{n-1}\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f_n=3 \\cdot 2^{n-1}\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f_n=3^n\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f_n=3+2n\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f_n=3+2^n\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A sequence is defined recursively by \(f_1=3\), \(f_n=2 \cdot f_{n-1}\), \(n\geq2\). Which of the following gives the \(n^{th}\) term for \(n\geq0\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(f_n=3 \cdot 2^{n-1}\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="ce6668d2-9242-4870-bca6-77ffc2b01910" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(4n-7\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(4n-3\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(4n-7\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(7n-4\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(3n-4\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A sequence of numbers is given by \(a_2=1\), \(a_3=5\), \(a_4=9\), \(a_5=13\), \(a_6=17\). Which expression can be used to find the \(n^{th}\) term of the sequence?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(4n-7\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q#7  Fixed -->
  <exercise data-content-id="4ff598c9-dc47-43e1-95a4-4660909eca7c" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(a_n = \frac{1}{27}(3)^{n-1}\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(a_n = \\frac{1}{27}-\\frac{1}{3}(n-1)\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(a_n = \\frac{1}{27}(\\frac{1}{3})^{n-1}\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(a_n = \\frac{1}{27}(3)^{n-1}\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(a_n = \\frac{1}{27}\\div3(n-1)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A sequence of numbers is given by \(\frac{1}{27}\), \(\frac19\), \(\frac13\), 1, …. What is the equation that can be used to find the \(n^{th}\) term of the sequence?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(a_n = \frac{1}{27}(3)^{n-1}\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q#8  Fixed -->
  <exercise data-content-id="534149b6-1201-4140-aeed-e7efc99df673" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(a_n = 5 + (n-1)(-4)\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(a_n = 5 + (n-1)(-4)\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(a_n = 5(-4)^{n-1}\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(a_n = 5(4)^{n-1}\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(a_n = 5 - (n-1)(-4)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A sequence is given by the set of numbers 5, 1, -3, -7 … What is the equation that can be used to find the \(n^{th}\) term of the sequence? </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(a_n = 5 + (n-1)(-4)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div>

<!--Do not edit this div tag.  It must surround the interaction block-->
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="d68983fe-520b-4f25-abde-5f8edccdd470" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <exercise data-content-id="a213731c-aa53-490d-a01a-b1b95c11851b" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="\(a_n = 1280 (\frac 14)^{n-1}\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(a_n = 1280 - 960(n-1)\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(a_n = 1280 (4)^{n-1}\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(a_n = 1280 (\\frac 14)^{n-1}\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(a_n = 1280 \\div 4(n-1)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A sequence consists of the following terms: 1280, 320, 80, … What is the explicit formula for this sequence? </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(a_n = 1280 (\frac 14)^{n-1}\) </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <exercise data-content-id="4756240a-7f43-40ce-930b-8c33a8a7c052" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="\(a_n = 1 +5(n-1)\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(a_n = 1 +5(n-1)\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(a_n = 1 (a_{n-1})^5\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(a_n = 5+ a_{n-1}\\)&quot;,&quot;\\(a_n = 1 + 5(a_{n-1})\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>An arithmetic sequence consists of the terms 1, 6, 11, 16 …. If you were asked to find the \(100^{th}\) term, what is the general formula you would use to find that value efficiently? </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(a_n = 1 +5(n-1)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.18.2 – 4.18.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div></section>
</content>
